FINDINGS:

1. [MAJOR] src/interpreter.rs:2666 (and src/bytecode/vm.rs:2886) — the new gate `if !from_module && stdlib::is_module_only_builtin(name)` runs before any user-function lookup, and `is_module_only_builtin` (src/stdlib.rs:1790) is an unconditional match on `text_wrap`/`text_diff`/`text_dedent`/`text_template`/`text_pad`/`text_align`. A user program that declares `to text_wrap(...)` (these are legal identifiers, like the existing `starts_with`/`random_seed` names) can no longer call it: the call is refused as `Unknown function 'text_wrap'` even though the declaration exists. Before phase-101 those names were not reserved, so this is a regression in the user-name surface introduced solely by the internal storage name. Fix: give the six internal builtins unspellable names (the existing `LRU_CACHE_DECLARE`/`\0` convention in src/stdlib.rs:1859) or have the gate run only after confirming no declared function/binding of that name exists. VERIFIED: traced the path — `Vm::call` reaches the guard at interpreter.rs:2666 before `builtin`/user-function resolution, and `is_module_only_builtin` has no declaration check.

2. [MINOR] src/stdlib.rs:1876 / 1897 and src/stdlib.rs:1777 — the six module-only names are inserted into the public `BUILTIN_NAMES` table and therefore `stdlib::is_builtin("text_wrap")` returns true. That makes `analyzer.rs:217` accept a bare `text_wrap` as a builtin name, the REPL completer/highlighter/introspection (repl/completer.rs:717, repl/highlight.rs:200, repl/introspect.rs:127) advertise it, and `set f to text_wrap` evaluate to `Value::Builtin("text_wrap")` — while both engines refuse to call it. The registry now advertises six names the language refuses, and SURVEY's "120 builtins" count includes them. Fix: keep these internal names out of the user-facing table (as the NUL-prefixed internal names already are) or filter them in `is_builtin` and the REPL surface queries.

3. [MINOR] src/stdlib.rs:1110 — `wrap_text` uses `line.split_whitespace()`, so it silently collapses runs of horizontal whitespace, converts tabs and other Unicode whitespace to a single space, and treats a bare `\r` as a separator. SPEC.md §text documents only that "existing newlines start a new line, CRLF is read as a line ending"; it never says horizontal whitespace is normalized. The behaviour is pinned by tests/text_utilities_test.rs:685 but not documented, so `text.wrap("a\tb", 10)` answering `["a b"]` is spec drift a reader cannot predict. Fix: either preserve horizontal whitespace (split on spaces only) or state the normalization rule in SPEC.md and README.md.

4. [MINOR] tests/text_utilities_test.rs:562 — REPORT.md's test matrix claims type-mismatch coverage includes "a non-text fill", but the type-mismatch test only exercises a non-numeric width for `pad` (`text.pad("x", "five", "-")`) and never a non-text fill such as `text.pad("x", 3, 1)`. The `Value::Text(fill)` arm in the `text_pad` destructuring (src/stdlib.rs:1014) is therefore untested on either engine. Add the non-text-fill case to the `both_refuse` loop, or correct the REPORT claim.

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 4
